{"name":"Brown Hare","hex":"#d3b793","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brown hare","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["European hare (Lepus europaeus)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown_hare"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cape hare","blocks":[{"text":"The Cape hare, also called the brown hare and the desert hare, is a hare native to Africa and Arabia extending into India."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lepus_capensis_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Lepus_capensis_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_hare"},{"title":"European hare","blocks":[{"text":"The European hare, also known as the brown hare, is a species of hare native to Europe and parts of Asia. It is among the largest hare species and is adapted to temperate, open country. Hares are herbivorous and feed mainly on grasses and herbs, supplementing these with twigs, buds, bark and field crops, particularly in winter. Their natural predators include red foxes and large birds of prey. They rely on high-speed endurance running to escape predation, having long, powerful limbs and large nostrils."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/European_hare_%28_Lepus_europaeus%29.jpg/330px-European_hare_%28_Lepus_europaeus%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_hare"},{"title":"Hare","blocks":[{"text":"Hares are mammals belonging to the genus Lepus. They are herbivores and live solitarily or in pairs. They nest in depressions in the ground called forms, and their young are able to fend for themselves shortly after birth. The genus includes the largest lagomorphs. Most are fast runners with long, powerful hind legs, and large ears that dissipate body heat. Hare species are native to Africa, Eurasia and North America. A hare less than one year old is called a leveret. A group of hares may be called either a husk or a down."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg/330px-Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hare"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Cape_Hare_area.png/330px-Cape_Hare_area.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Cape_Hare_area.png/330px-Cape_Hare_area.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACape_Hare_area.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Kushki_%26_rubit.jpg/500px-Kushki_%26_rubit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Kushki_%26_rubit.jpg/500px-Kushki_%26_rubit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Cape hare caught by an Asiatic cheetah in Miandasht Wildlife Refuge, Iran.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKushki_%26_rubit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/European_Hare_area.png/500px-European_Hare_area.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or